.TH du "" "Command"
.SH NAME
du \- summarize disk usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdu [\-a] [\-s] [\fIdirectory ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
du -- summarize disk usage
Usage:	du [ options ] [ directory ... ]
Options:
	-a	Print an entry for each file
	-s	Print only a summary
.HE
For each named
.IR directory ,
.B du
walks the directory tree rooted there and prints, in 512-byte blocks,
the space used by each subdirectory (as a running subtotal, most
deeply nested first), followed by the grand total for
.I directory
itself.
If no
.I directory
is given,
.B du
reports on the current directory.
If a named argument is a plain file rather than a directory,
.B du
prints its size and nothing else.
.IP \fB\-a\fR 0.5i
Also print a line for every plain file, not just for directories.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Print only the single grand total for each named
.I directory
(or file); suppresses the per-subdirectory and, if
.B \-a
was also given, per-file lines.
.PP
.B du
counts a file that has more than one link to it only once, using an
internal table of up to 500 (device, i-number) pairs already seen; if
a tree contains more distinct multiply-linked files than that,
.B du
warns and the excess ones are counted more than once.
.PP
Because each level of directory recursion holds a file descriptor and
some stack, a directory tree nested more than 12 levels deep is
finished by a forked child process rather than by further recursion
in the same process; this is normal and not reported as an error.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B du
prints a warning and skips (counting it as zero blocks) any directory
it cannot open or a directory entry it cannot
.BR stat() ,
and a ``directory structure too deep to traverse'' warning if a path
name grows too long to hold, or if forking to continue a deeply
nested tree fails.
None of these warnings changes the exit status, which is 0 unless
.B du
was given an unrecognized option.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR df ,
.B find
.SH NOTES
.B du
does not account for indirect blocks or other file-system overhead,
so its totals can under-report the space a tree actually occupies on
a device.
